    "answer": {
      "short": "24 oranges",
      "worked": "Let the original numbers of apples and oranges be $x$ and $3x$. After Ed adds the apples, there are $x+4$ apples and $3x$ oranges.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{x+4}{3x}&=\\frac{1}{2}\\\\x+4&=\\frac{1}{2}(3x)\\\\2(x+4)&=3x\\\\2x+8&=3x\\\\8&=x\\end{align*}$$\nNumber of oranges $=3x=3(8)=24$.\nThere are 24 oranges.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
